            <ArticleTitle>Drug-eluting stents and the future of coronary artery bypass surgery: facts and fiction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1162-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The treatment of patients with coronary artery disease continues to evolve. Recent, exciting data on the use of drug-eluting stents in diseased coronary vessels has generated immense enthusiasm within the interventional community leading to claims that &quot;drug-eluting stents will put bypass surgeons out of business.&quot; However, despite promising short-term and midterm outcomes of this revolutionary new technology, valid concerns regarding long-term safety and efficacy of drug-eluting stents persist. This review article evaluates current status of drug-eluting stents with special emphasis on real and potential drawbacks of this emerging percutaneous coronary interventional modality and its impact on the practice of coronary artery bypass surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
